[[Open This|journalctl]]

---

```bash
journalctl -b                          # current boot only
journalctl -b -1                       # previous boot
journalctl --since "1 hour ago"
journalctl --since "2026-11-10 08:00" --until "2026-11-10 09:00"
journalctl -p err -b                   # priority err and worse, this boot
journalctl -k                          # kernel messages (like dmesg)
journalctl -f                          # follow live (Ctrl+C to quit)
journalctl -u ssh.service -n 20 --no-pager
journalctl -o json-pretty -n 1         # see the structured fields
```

Field matching is where it gets powerful:

```bash
journalctl _SYSTEMD_UNIT=ssh.service
journalctl _UID=1000
journalctl _COMM=sudo
journalctl /usr/bin/sudo 
```

4. Housekeeping commands worth knowing
bash
systemctl --failed                     # all failed units, a great first command on an unknown box
sudo journalctl --vacuum-time=2weeks   # prune old logs (this deletes data, so only if you want to)
sudo journalctl --verify               # check journal integrity

Reading /etc/systemd/journald.conf is safe. Changing it (Storage=, SystemMaxUse=) is what you’d do to make logs persistent or cap their size, but leave it alone for now.

Interview-ready workflow

When someone says “the service won’t start”:

systemctl status <unit> for the state and exit code
journalctl -u <unit> -b for the full story this boot
journalctl -p err -b in case the cause is outside the unit (disk, network, kernel)
systemctl cat <unit> to confirm what’s actually configured, including drop-ins